# AD Attack Detection — Coverage Overview

Comparison of our current detection coverage against the 17 Active Directory attack techniques in *Detecting and mitigating Active Directory compromises* (ASD, with CISA, NSA, CCCS, NCSC-UK, NCSC-NZ — September 2026).

## What we have today

**44 detection rules total — 43 Splunk, 1 Defender.**

Mapped to MITRE, the Splunk content is heavily concentrated rather than broad. The large majority are ESCU PowerShell rules mapping to T1059.001 (Command and Scripting Interpreter: PowerShell), with the remainder spread thinly across process injection, WMI, masquerading and a handful of others.

Of all 44 rules, **one** maps to a technique in this guidance:

| Rule | Technique | Covers |
|---|---|---|
| Active Directory multiple failed logon attempts from external IP | T1110.003 | Password spraying |

Full inventory and MITRE mappings are in the attached spreadsheet, with the ATT&CK Navigator layer linked separately.

That figure isn't as alarming as it sounds, because Defender for Identity covers most of these techniques natively without us writing anything. But it does mean that if Defender is degraded, we have almost nothing of our own behind it.

